{:id 200 :ts "2026-07-24T14:00:00Z" :answers-q [9] :owner "claude"
 :answer "REFUTES the framing of q9/A7: this is NOT a post-birth integrator fling at all (the universal-compact-substepping fix from cbe80dd is real, live, and working as designed for EXISTING bodies). It is a SPAWN-SEAM staleness bug. Runtime proof (scratchpad/probe_fling2.clj, probe_fling3.clj, probe_fling4.clj, probe_fling5.clj, seed 42): eid 1001 is already at |rel-v|=43518.5 m/s (~11.6x escape) the FIRST tick it is observed (tick 4040) at only 35.9 AU from its star (491) -- the velocity anomaly exists at birth, not accumulated over ticks 4040->4100 (probe_fling.out shows |v| barely changes tick-to-tick after that -- it is free straight-line flight, confirmed by dist growing ~1200 AU/tick matching |rel-v|*dt exactly). Calling domain.planet-formation.seed/planet-seeds directly on the pre-spawn-tick world (probe_fling4.clj) shows the SPEC itself is self-consistent and CORRECTLY bound (position 0.1304 AU, velocity paired to that same radius). The bug is in materialize-lifecycle timing: domain.genesis.tick/tick-physics (tick.clj:154-163) runs step-physics (the integrator INCLUDED, which already advances the star's own position/velocity for THIS tick) BEFORE bootstrap/materialize-lifecycle. probe_fling5.clj proves it numerically: star 491 moved 35.18 AU in the single tick between the frozen snapshot used to build the spec and the post-physics world materialize-lifecycle runs on (v_star=548 m/s * dt=9.8e9s dt ~ 310yr = 35.18 AU, matching design doc's own §3.0 warning almost exactly, '5-20 AU per tick' -- here even larger). The planet's absolute :position/:velocity were baked in against the star's PRE-tick position; by materialization the star has already moved ~35 AU away, so the planet is born already far outside any bound orbit relative to where the star ACTUALLY is. This is the §3.0 stale-anchor class the multi-timescale design fixed for the ONGOING integrator (via relative/Jacobian coordinates), reborn at the ONE place that design never touched: spawn."
